WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.050 --> 00:00:05.970
Bird Box may have terrified you with its chilling,
post-apocalyptic setting, but it was originally

00:00:05.970 --> 00:00:08.170
going to be even darker!

00:00:08.170 --> 00:00:12.530
Yippee-ki-yay movie lovers, I'm Jan and in
this video I'm revealing the horrific alternate

00:00:12.530 --> 00:00:18.099
ending for Bird Box, as well as another terrifying
river scene you never got to see, plus a deleted

00:00:18.099 --> 00:00:21.060
scene that actually showed the creatures on
screen!

00:00:21.060 --> 00:00:24.159
Spoilers ahead so take care if you haven't
seen the movie yet.

00:00:24.159 --> 00:00:29.609
At the end of Bird Box, Malorie, Boy and Girl
survive the rapids despite being capsized,

00:00:29.609 --> 00:00:34.210
but then end up separated in the woods where
they're tempted by the creatures to remove

00:00:34.210 --> 00:00:35.210
their blindfolds.

00:00:35.210 --> 00:00:39.260
Terrorised and chased by the monsters, they
flee towards the School For The Blind, where

00:00:39.260 --> 00:00:41.420
they eventually find safety.

00:00:41.420 --> 00:00:45.649
In the original script, however, as the monsters
chase them through the trees, Boy and Girl

00:00:45.649 --> 00:00:49.440
make it to the door, but Malorie trips and
gets left behind.

00:00:49.440 --> 00:00:54.679
Boy and Girl get taken in safely by Rick,
but Malorie – who's stranded – is flipped

00:00:54.679 --> 00:00:56.550
over by one of the creatures.

00:00:56.550 --> 00:01:01.230
The creatures' shadows "fall over her face"
and Malorie fights back, telling the malevolent

00:01:01.230 --> 00:01:02.910
forces to "stay away".

00:01:02.910 --> 00:01:08.600
She pulls out her knife, then in a horrifying
moment, puts the knife's tip right next to

00:01:08.600 --> 00:01:12.400
her eyelid, warning the creatures that she'll
make sure she can't look at them.

00:01:12.400 --> 00:01:16.980
The creatures buzz around her and, in a close-up
shot, Malorie pricks her eye with the knife

00:01:16.980 --> 00:01:19.230
drawing a drop of blood.

00:01:19.230 --> 00:01:24.220
Seeing this, the creatures all move back,
and she's finally able to get inside the building.

00:01:24.220 --> 00:01:28.170
This horrific moment would have tipped its
hat to the end of Josh Malerman's book on

00:01:28.170 --> 00:01:31.600
which the movie's based and which is also
much darker.

00:01:31.600 --> 00:01:36.040
Spoiler for the novel now: In the book's ending,
after Malorie arrives at the School For The

00:01:36.040 --> 00:01:39.780
Blind, she discovers that many of the residents
have gouged out their own eyes so they can't

00:01:39.780 --> 00:01:41.340
be tempted by the monsters.

00:01:41.340 --> 00:01:45.950
In the novel, Rick tells Malorie that previously
the school's security was much weaker and

00:01:45.950 --> 00:01:48.490
a creature managed to break into the building.

00:01:48.490 --> 00:01:53.500
During the attack, many people died and a
mother "killed four children in a fit of rage".

00:01:53.500 --> 00:01:57.990
The community decided they couldn't risk it
happening again, so they blinded themselves

00:01:57.990 --> 00:02:03.079
with various implements including forks, kitchen
knives and even their own fingers.

00:02:03.079 --> 00:02:07.020
The original screenplay has a similar scene
that happens earlier but was cut from the

00:02:07.020 --> 00:02:12.320
final film, where one night Malorie almost
blinds her son deliberately with a hypodermic

00:02:12.320 --> 00:02:13.320
needle.

00:02:13.320 --> 00:02:17.620
The scene takes place at night and Malorie
is terrified as she can hear the wind or creatures

00:02:17.620 --> 00:02:19.430
bumping against the house.

00:02:19.430 --> 00:02:23.850
The two children are asleep covered with blankets,
but Malorie has reached the point where she's

00:02:23.850 --> 00:02:27.430
decided that blinding them is the only hope
for their survival.

00:02:27.430 --> 00:02:31.660
Malorie places the needle right up close to
Boy's eye, but can't go through with it after

00:02:31.660 --> 00:02:33.500
Girl wakes up startled.

00:02:33.500 --> 00:02:38.320
Bird Box director Susanne Bier has said, in
an interview with Polygon, that when she decided

00:02:38.320 --> 00:02:43.240
to make a movie about the apocalypse, it was
important to her that there be a hopeful ending.

00:02:43.240 --> 00:02:46.630
Bier says she didn't want "the audience to
leave […] with a [...] completely bleak

00:02:46.630 --> 00:02:47.710
point of view.

00:02:47.710 --> 00:02:51.380
[…] And so […] it was key […] that […] this
scary, dystopian story, […] has a hopeful

00:02:51.380 --> 00:02:52.380
undercurrent."

00:02:52.380 --> 00:02:57.410
Certainly a version of Bird Box with Malorie
almost gouging out her own eyes, very nearly

00:02:57.410 --> 00:03:01.770
blinding her son on purpose, or discovering
a community of people who've clawed out their

00:03:01.770 --> 00:03:07.090
eyes as the only remedy against the monsters
is much bleaker even if her family does still

00:03:07.090 --> 00:03:08.090
survive.

00:03:08.090 --> 00:03:12.100
Indeed, speaking to the Hollywood Reporter,
Bier also said that she "cannot do horror"

00:03:12.100 --> 00:03:16.570
and is "much more interested in suspense and
thrillers", preferring "things that are threatening

00:03:16.570 --> 00:03:17.950
but not gory".

00:03:17.950 --> 00:03:23.160
Which seems to be why some of the more gory
elements of the story have been toned down.

00:03:23.160 --> 00:03:27.599
Unlike many horror or monster movies, Bird
Box decides not to show us what the creatures

00:03:27.599 --> 00:03:33.140
look like beyond shadows passing over surfaces
and how the wind blows and leaves swirl when

00:03:33.140 --> 00:03:34.140
they move.

00:03:34.140 --> 00:03:38.280
However, there was a moment in the original
script which was also filmed where Malorie

00:03:38.280 --> 00:03:40.850
has a nightmare about seeing one of the creatures.

00:03:40.850 --> 00:03:44.960
The script describes a scene where Malorie
is making a phone call and a creature creeps

00:03:44.960 --> 00:03:46.290
up behind her.

00:03:46.290 --> 00:03:51.240
She hears it and shuts her eyes, trying to
hide, but the monster stretches out "two ragged

00:03:51.240 --> 00:03:56.739
fingers with wet, black talons on the ends
[which] press against her cheek and eyebrow".

00:03:56.739 --> 00:04:01.510
Similar to the final film where Gary forces
Cheryl to look, in the deleted scene, the

00:04:01.510 --> 00:04:06.330
creature forces Malorie's eyes open and she
sees "a wide-mouthed, pale, wet face with

00:04:06.330 --> 00:04:08.910
bulging eyes like a deep-sea monster".

00:04:08.910 --> 00:04:13.140
Malorie then wakes up petrified from her nightmare
and goes downstairs for some water.

00:04:13.140 --> 00:04:17.799
In the script, this is the moment when Gary
arrives at the house and it's Malorie, not

00:04:17.799 --> 00:04:19.559
Olympia, who lets him in.

00:04:19.559 --> 00:04:24.439
In an interview with Bloody Disgusting, screenwriter
Eric Heisserer says he only added the nightmare

00:04:24.439 --> 00:04:28.039
scene with the creature because "there was
a time when one of the producers [… said]

00:04:28.039 --> 00:04:31.120
'you have to see something at some point'".

00:04:31.120 --> 00:04:35.270
Director Susanne Bier said that they actually
spent a lot of time filming the scene, but

00:04:35.270 --> 00:04:39.560
what happened was that when they reviewed
the footage it just ended up being funny rather

00:04:39.560 --> 00:04:42.780
than scary, which, of course, was the opposite
of what they wanted.

00:04:42.780 --> 00:04:47.110
Sandra Bullock has described the look of the
creature in the deleted footage as "snake-like"

00:04:47.110 --> 00:04:49.139
and resembling "a long fat baby".

00:04:49.139 --> 00:04:53.729
Now, some people might like to have seen a
creature to get an idea of what they looked

00:04:53.729 --> 00:04:58.469
like, but leaving something unseen and to
the imagination can often be very scary and

00:04:58.469 --> 00:05:02.270
effective, especially when compared to a poorly-done
CGI-monster.

00:05:02.270 --> 00:05:08.029
In comparison, A Quiet Place only showed bits
of its creatures which also worked fantastically

00:05:08.029 --> 00:05:10.490
well to create a real sense of dread.

00:05:10.490 --> 00:05:15.409
Also, if they had kept the deleted monster
scene in Bird Box, it would have made it extra

00:05:15.409 --> 00:05:19.449
obvious that Gary was going to be in league
with the creatures and, on top of that, you

00:05:19.449 --> 00:05:23.759
would also likely have worked out pretty quickly
that the creature was just a nightmare as

00:05:23.759 --> 00:05:27.780
you'd know the filmmakers would be rather
unlikely to kill off the lead.

00:05:27.780 --> 00:05:32.229
Bier has also said the idea of the creatures
in Bird Box is that they tap into your deepest

00:05:32.229 --> 00:05:36.550
fear, but as everyone's fears are different,
if you give the monsters a definitive form

00:05:36.550 --> 00:05:39.789
in the movie, it might take away from that
concept.

00:05:39.789 --> 00:05:44.080
In the end, we still have Gary's sketches
as examples of what the creatures might look

00:05:44.080 --> 00:05:45.180
like.

00:05:45.180 --> 00:05:50.300
The original script for Bird Box also reveals
other deleted scenes that were rather dark.

00:05:50.300 --> 00:05:54.279
A particularly interesting and terrifying
one is an alternate version of the final river

00:05:54.279 --> 00:05:55.279
scene.

00:05:55.279 --> 00:05:59.150
In the final movie, just before Malorie and
the children reach the rapids, Malorie is

00:05:59.150 --> 00:06:03.780
faced with the dilemma of asking one of the
kids to look so that she can safely navigate

00:06:03.780 --> 00:06:05.300
the river at that point.

00:06:05.300 --> 00:06:10.319
She realizes eventually that it's an impossible
choice, and instead decides to do everything

00:06:10.319 --> 00:06:11.999
she can so they all survive.

00:06:11.999 --> 00:06:15.759
In the original script, though, we get a horrific
scene at this point that feels inspired by

00:06:15.759 --> 00:06:18.289
Hitchcock's classic film The Birds.

00:06:18.289 --> 00:06:21.970
As Malorie and the children float down the
river and Malorie tries to figure out what

00:06:21.970 --> 00:06:26.479
to do, a flock of migrating birds pass overhead
squawking normally.

00:06:26.479 --> 00:06:30.850
But as they approach the rapids, the sound
from the birds becomes more aggressive and

00:06:30.850 --> 00:06:32.499
they start attacking each other.

00:06:32.499 --> 00:06:37.280
Dead birds begin falling into the river and
their blood splashes onto Malorie's face.

00:06:37.280 --> 00:06:42.389
A bird then comes crashing into the boat as
it tries to kill itself; the boat hits a rock

00:06:42.389 --> 00:06:45.400
and the budgies inside the box start chirping.

00:06:45.400 --> 00:06:49.990
Malorie hears the sound of a monster nearby
and grabbing an oar pokes it in the direction

00:06:49.990 --> 00:06:53.349
of the creature which retaliates by capsizing
the boat.

00:06:53.349 --> 00:06:56.939
This scene would have replaced the scene with
the rapids that cause the boat to capsize

00:06:56.939 --> 00:07:00.919
in the final movie, and it would also have
skipped over Malorie having to decide which

00:07:00.919 --> 00:07:04.270
child would face the mortal danger of being
the look-out.

00:07:04.270 --> 00:07:08.409
Like the alternate ending, a suicidal bird
attack would have been a rather shocking scene,

00:07:08.409 --> 00:07:13.160
so I can see why this wasn't included if the
director wanted to tone down some of the story's

00:07:13.160 --> 00:07:14.330
darkness.

00:07:14.330 --> 00:07:18.830
And showing Malorie deciding to save both
children adds a touch more optimism to the

00:07:18.830 --> 00:07:19.830
final movie.

00:07:19.830 --> 00:07:23.649
So, do you wish any of these deleted scenes
had been in the movie?

00:07:23.649 --> 00:07:27.240
And what are your theories about the monsters
in Bird Box?

